{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guangyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MSR-Codec: A Low-Bitrate Multi-Stream Residual Codec for High-Fidelity Speech Generation with Information Disentanglement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:22:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:46:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T05:45:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:27:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T08:22:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:31:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Audio codecs are a critical component of modern speech generation systems. This paper introduces a low-bitrate, multi-scale residual codec that encodes speech into four distinct streams: semantic, timbre, prosody, and residual. This architecture achieves high-fidelity speech reconstruction at competitive low bitrates while demonstrating an inherent ability for information disentanglement. We construct a two-stage language model for text-to-speech (TTS) synthesis using this codec, which, despite its lightweight design and minimal data requirements, achieves a state-of-the-art Word Error Rate (WER) and superior speaker similarity compared to several larger models. Furthermore, the codec's design proves highly effective for voice conversion, enabling independent manipulation of speaker timbre and prosody. Our inference code, pre-trained models, and audio samples are available at https://github.com/herbertLJY/MSRCodec."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:55:04Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:55:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:08:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12456","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12456","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12456","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Zimmer","name":"Zimmer, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oswaldo Luiz do Valle","familyName":"Costa","name":"Costa, Oswaldo Luiz do Valle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning-Based Market Making as a Stochastic Control on Non-Stationary Limit Order Book Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Trading and Market Microstructure (q-fin.TR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T21:08:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:09:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-14T18:57:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:37:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning has emerged as a promising framework for developing adaptive and data-driven strategies, enabling market makers to optimize decision-making policies based on interactions with the limit order book environment. This paper explores the integration of a reinforcement learning agent in a market-making context, where the underlying market dynamics have been explicitly modeled to capture observed stylized facts of real markets, including clustered order arrival times, non-stationary spreads and return drifts, stochastic order quantities and price volatility. These mechanisms aim to enhance stability of the resulting control agent, and serve to incorporate domain-specific knowledge into the agent policy learning process. Our contributions include a practical implementation of a market making agent based on the Proximal-Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm, alongside a comparative evaluation of the agent's performance under varying market conditions via a simulator-based environment. As evidenced by our analysis of the financial return and risk metrics when compared to a closed-form optimal solution, our results suggest that the reinforcement learning agent can effectively be used under non-stationary market conditions, and that the proposed simulator-based environment can serve as a valuable tool for training and pre-training reinforcement learning agents in market-making scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 8 figures, 3 tables, 31 equations"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12456","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:50Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:44:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13148","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13148","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13148","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jung-Woo","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Jung-Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Large Audio Language Models Understand Audio Well? Speech, Scene and Events Understanding Benchmark for LALMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:05:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:52:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T11:57:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:37:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T16:26:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:51:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) have progressed rapidly, demonstrating their strong efficacy in universal audio understanding through cross-modal integration. To evaluate LALMs' audio understanding performance, researchers have proposed different benchmarks. However, key aspects for real-world interactions are underexplored in existing benchmarks, i.e., audio signals typically contain both speech and non-speech components, and energy levels of these components can vary significantly across different scenarios. Moreover, most benchmarks do not consider the joint understanding of speech, scene, and events within the same audio clip. In this work, we introduce SSEU-Bench, the first versatile audio understanding benchmark that explicitly accounts for energy differences between speech and non-speech audio, with both independent and joint understanding settings for speech, scene, and events. Furthermore, we demonstrate that some LALMs tend to underperform on certain tasks in a joint understanding setting. To address this issue, we introduce Chain-of-Thought, which effectively improves LALMs' joint audio understanding performance by decomposing complex tasks into simpler reasoning steps."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13148","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:57:01Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:57:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-16T02:59:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12365","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12365","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12365","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Jreissaty","name":"Jreissaty, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jairo C.","familyName":"Quijano","name":"Quijano, Jairo C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Carrasquilla","name":"Carrasquilla, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roeland","familyName":"Wiersema","name":"Wiersema, Roeland","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Entanglement and optimization within autoregressive neural quantum states"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:59:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:04:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T19:32:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:02:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T18:54:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-11T02:12:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/t2cg-kr7y","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural quantum states (NQSs) are powerful variational ansätze capable of representing highly entangled quantum many-body wavefunctions. While the average entanglement properties of ensembles of restricted Boltzmann machines are well understood, the entanglement structure of autoregressive NQSs such as recurrent neural networks and transformers remains largely unexplored. We perform large-scale simulations of ensembles of random autoregressive wavefunctions for chains of up to $256$ spins and uncover signatures of transitions in their average entanglement scaling, entanglement spectra, and correlation functions. We show that the standard softmax normalization of the wavefunction suppresses entanglement and fluctuations, and introduce a square modulus normalization function that restores them. Finally, we connect the insights gained from our entanglement and activation function analysis to initialization strategies for finding the ground states of strongly correlated Hamiltonians via variational Monte Carlo."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 17 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12365","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:47Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-11T03:28:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zelei","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Zelei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Building Coding Agents via Entropy-Enhanced Multi-Turn Preference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:36:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:07:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T01:28:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:43:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T05:16:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:27:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Software engineering presents complex, multi-step challenges for Large Language Models (LLMs), requiring reasoning over large codebases and coordinated tool use. The difficulty of these tasks is exemplified by benchmarks like SWE-bench, where current LLMs still struggle to resolve real-world issues. A promising approach to enhance performance is test-time scaling (TTS), but its gains are heavily dependent on the diversity of model outputs. While standard alignment methods such as Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) and Kahneman-Tversky Optimization (KTO) are effective at aligning model outputs with human preferences, this process can come at the cost of reduced diversity, limiting the effectiveness of TTS. Additionally, existing preference optimization algorithms are typically designed for single-turn tasks and do not fully address the complexities of multi-turn reasoning and tool integration required for interactive coding agents. To bridge this gap, we introduce EntroPO, an entropy-enhanced framework that adapts existing preference optimization algorithms to the multi-turn, tool-assisted setting. EntroPO augments the preference objective to explicitly preserve policy entropy and generalizes learning to optimize over multi-turn interactions rather than single-turn responses. We validate EntroPO by fine-tuning a diverse suite of models from different families and sizes (up to 106B parameters).To maximize performance gains from TTS, we further propose a hybrid best-trajectory selection scheme combining a learned verifier model with model free approaches. On the SWEBENCH leaderboard, our approach establishes new state-of-the-art results among open-weight models. A 30B parameter model trained with EntroPO ranks 1st on SWEBENCH-LITE and 4th on SWEBENCH-VERIFIED on the open-weight leaderboard, surpassed only by models with over 10x more parameters(e.g., \u0026gt;$350B)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:20Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-05T03:20:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12517","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12517","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12517","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shomik","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Shomik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charlotte","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Charlotte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matt","familyName":"Viana","name":"Viana, Matt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashia","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, Ashia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dana","familyName":"Calacci","name":"Calacci, Dana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interaction Context Often Increases Sycophancy in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:41:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:12:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-07T18:25:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:58:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T15:18:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T02:07:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3791915","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate how the presence and type of interaction context shapes sycophancy in LLMs. While real-world interactions allow models to mirror a user's values, preferences, and self-image, prior work often studies sycophancy in zero-shot settings devoid of context. Using two weeks of interaction context from 38 users, we evaluate two forms of sycophancy: (1) agreement sycophancy -- the tendency of models to produce overly affirmative responses, and (2) perspective sycophancy -- the extent to which models reflect a user's viewpoint. Agreement sycophancy tends to increase with the presence of user context, though model behavior varies based on the context type. User memory profiles are associated with the largest increases in agreement sycophancy (e.g. $+$45\\% for Gemini 2.5 Pro), and some models become more sycophantic even with non-user synthetic contexts (e.g. $+$15\\% for Llama 4 Scout). Perspective sycophancy increases only when models can accurately infer user viewpoints from interaction context. Overall, context shapes sycophancy in heterogeneous ways, underscoring the need for evaluations grounded in real-world interactions and raising questions for system design around alignment, memory, and personalization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the proceedings of the ACM Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems (CHI 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12517","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:13Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:40:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ya","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujie","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Performance Gaps for ECG Foundation Models: A Post-Training Strategy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:42:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T12:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"ECG foundation models are increasingly popular due to their adaptability across various tasks. However, their clinical applicability is often limited by performance gaps compared to task-specific models, even after pre-training on large ECG datasets and fine-tuning on target data. This limitation is likely due to the lack of an effective post-training strategy. In this paper, we propose a simple yet effective post-training approach to enhance ECG foundation models. We evaluate it on a publicly available Transformer-based foundation model. Experiments across multiple ECG tasks show that our method consistently outperforms baseline fine-tuning. On the PTB-XL benchmarks, it improves macro AUROC by 0.7%-8.9% and macro AUPRC by 23.3%-77.9%, also outperforming several recent state-of-the-art approaches, including task-specific and advanced architectures. Further analyses demonstrate improved training dynamics and data efficiency, with only 30% of the training data outperforming the baseline trained on the full dataset. Ablation studies highlight the importance of stochastic depth and preview linear probing. These findings underscore the potential of post-training strategies to improve ECG foundation models, and we hope this work will contribute to the continued development of foundation models in the ECG domain."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"A Transformer-based model is used as an example; the proposed post-training strategy may also be applicable to CNN-based models. The manuscript is currently under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:53:17Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:53:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:41:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siju","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Siju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changsiyu","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Changsiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengjie","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Chengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RIS-FUSION: Rethinking Text-Driven Infrared and Visible Image Fusion from the Perspective of Referring Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T06:03:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:25:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-19T15:19:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:31:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-driven infrared and visible image fusion has gained attention for enabling natural language to guide the fusion process. However, existing methods lack a goal-aligned task to supervise and evaluate how effectively the input text contributes to the fusion outcome. We observe that referring image segmentation (RIS) and text-driven fusion share a common objective: highlighting the object referred to by the text. Motivated by this, we propose RIS-FUSION, a cascaded framework that unifies fusion and RIS through joint optimization. At its core is the LangGatedFusion module, which injects textual features into the fusion backbone to enhance semantic alignment. To support multimodal referring image segmentation task, we introduce MM-RIS, a large-scale benchmark with 12.5k training and 3.5k testing triplets, each consisting of an infrared-visible image pair, a segmentation mask, and a referring expression. Extensive experiments show that RIS-FUSION achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming existing methods by over 11% in mIoU. Code and dataset will be released at https://github.com/SijuMa2003/RIS-FUSION."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:42Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:04:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12330","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12330","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12330","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raissa F. P.","familyName":"Mendes","name":"Mendes, Raissa F. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amanda","familyName":"Guerrieri","name":"Guerrieri, Amanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"João V. M.","familyName":"Muniz","name":"Muniz, João V. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel S.","familyName":"Rocha","name":"Rocha, Gabriel S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel S.","familyName":"Denicol","name":"Denicol, Gabriel S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Radial Oscillations of Viscous Neutron Stars: Zero Diffusion Case"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:01:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-08T13:08:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:42:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/fs15-pj7m","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The spectrum of radial oscillations of neutron stars is systematically studied within two frameworks of viscous relativistic hydrodynamics: the relativistic Navier-Stokes and Israel-Stewart theories. A correspondence is established between the discrete stellar eigenmodes and the continuous dispersion relation of perturbations around a homogeneous fluid, providing a basis for interpreting our numerical results. We analyze the Newtonian limit and assess the impact of relativistic corrections, such as the gravitational redshifting of microscopic relaxation timescales. We show that bulk viscosity can significantly affect the behavior of both hydrodynamic and nonhydrodynamic fundamental modes, and that, depending on the magnitude of the viscous effects, it is the nonhydrodynamic mode that becomes unstable beyond the turning point in a sequence of equilibrium configurations. These results provide a useful step toward systematic studies of neutron star quasinormal modes in the presence of viscosity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12330","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:37:58Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-09T03:10:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12421","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12421","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12421","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hicham","familyName":"Masri","name":"Masri, Hicham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filipe R.","familyName":"Cogo","name":"Cogo, Filipe R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdul Ali","familyName":"Bangash","name":"Bangash, Abdul Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bram","familyName":"Adams","name":"Adams, Bram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed E.","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Ahmed E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Prompt Management in GitHub Repositories: A Call for Best Practices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:18:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:07:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-11T20:00:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:05:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-04T00:37:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:29:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ms.2025.3644251","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid adoption of foundation models (e.g., large language models) has given rise to promptware, i.e., software built using natural language prompts. Effective management of prompts, such as organization and quality assurance, is essential yet challenging. In this study, we perform an empirical analysis of 24,800 open-source prompts from 92 GitHub repositories to investigate prompt management practices and quality attributes. Our findings reveal critical challenges such as considerable inconsistencies in prompt formatting, substantial internal and external prompt duplication, and frequent readability and spelling issues. Based on these findings, we provide actionable recommendations for developers to enhance the usability and maintainability of open-source prompts within the rapidly evolving promptware ecosystem."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12421","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:02Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:09:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12382","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12382","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12382","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anu","familyName":"Pradhan","name":"Pradhan, Anu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Ortan","name":"Ortan, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Apurv","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Apurv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhavan","familyName":"Seshadri","name":"Seshadri, Madhavan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-as-a-Judge: Rapid Evaluation of Legal Document Recommendation for Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.3; I.2.7; I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:20:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-30T01:18:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The evaluation bottleneck in recommendation systems has become particularly acute with the rise of Generative AI, where traditional metrics fall short of capturing nuanced quality dimensions that matter in specialized domains like legal research. Can we trust Large Language Models to serve as reliable judges of their own kind? This paper investigates LLM-as-a-Judge as a principled approach to evaluating Retrieval-Augmented Generation systems in legal contexts, where the stakes of recommendation quality are exceptionally high.\n We tackle two fundamental questions that determine practical viability: which inter-rater reliability metrics best capture the alignment between LLM and human assessments, and how do we conduct statistically sound comparisons between competing systems? Through systematic experimentation, we discover that traditional agreement metrics like Krippendorff's alpha can be misleading in the skewed distributions typical of AI system evaluations. Instead, Gwet's AC2 and rank correlation coefficients emerge as more robust indicators for judge selection, while the Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test with Benjamini-Hochberg corrections provides the statistical rigor needed for reliable system comparisons.\n Our findings suggest a path toward scalable, cost-effective evaluation that maintains the precision demanded by legal applications, transforming what was once a human-intensive bottleneck into an automated, yet statistically principled, evaluation framework."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in EARL 25: The 2nd Workshop on Evaluating and Applying Recommender Systems with Large Language Models at RecSys 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12382","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:10Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-30T03:50:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12416","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12416","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12416","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kentaro","familyName":"Nakamura","name":"Nakamura, Kentaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surrogate Representation Inference for Text and Image Annotations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:09:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:06:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-25T02:54:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-29T01:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As researchers increasingly rely on machine learning models and LLMs to annotate unstructured data, such as texts or images, various approaches have been proposed to correct bias in downstream statistical analysis. However, existing methods tend to yield large standard errors and require some error-free human annotation. In this paper, I introduce Surrogate Representation Inference (SRI), which assumes that unstructured data fully mediate the relationship between human annotations and structured variables. The assumption is guaranteed by design provided that human coders rely only on unstructured data for annotation. Under this setting, I propose a neural network architecture that learns a low-dimensional representation of unstructured data such that the surrogate assumption remains to be satisfied. When multiple human annotations are available, SRI can be extended to further correct non-differential measurement errors that may exist in human annotations. Focusing on text-as-outcome settings, I formally establish the identification conditions and semiparametric efficient estimation strategies that enable learning and leveraging such a low-dimensional representation. Simulation studies and a real-world application demonstrate that SRI reduces standard errors by over 50% when machine learning classification accuracy is moderate and provides valid inference even when human annotations contain non-differential measurement errors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12416","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:55Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-29T02:42:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12715","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12715","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12715","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haichuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Haichuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaomin","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yaomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilei","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yilei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"You","name":"You, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guo","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Guo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lubin","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Lubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huihui","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Huihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AsyMoE: Leveraging Modal Asymmetry for Enhanced Expert Specialization in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T06:16:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:26:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-22T18:22:20Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; This submission has been withdrawn by the authors due to a fundamental error in the methodology that affects the validity of the main results"},{"date":"2025-12-23T02:38:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have demonstrated impressive performance on multimodal tasks through scaled architectures and extensive training. However, existing Mixture of Experts (MoE) approaches face challenges due to the asymmetry between visual and linguistic processing. Visual information is spatially complete, while language requires maintaining sequential context. As a result, MoE models struggle to balance modality-specific features and cross-modal interactions. Through systematic analysis, we observe that language experts in deeper layers progressively lose contextual grounding and rely more on parametric knowledge rather than utilizing the provided visual and linguistic information. To address this, we propose AsyMoE, a novel architecture that models this asymmetry using three specialized expert groups. We design intra-modality experts for modality-specific processing, hyperbolic inter-modality experts for hierarchical cross-modal interactions, and evidence-priority language experts to suppress parametric biases and maintain contextual grounding. Extensive experiments demonstrate that AsyMoE achieves 26.58% and 15.45% accuracy improvements over vanilla MoE and modality-specific MoE respectively, with 25.45% fewer activated parameters than dense models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This submission has been withdrawn by the authors due to a fundamental error in the methodology that affects the validity of the main results"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12715","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:49Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-23T04:23:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12508","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12508","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12508","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"An","name":"An, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanni","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhigao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhigao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changfeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Changfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifu","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zhifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiangang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yabin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yabin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunjie","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yunjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiheng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoneng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haoneng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongjia","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Chongjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexu","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zexu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhendong","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiyao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wupeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biao","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Biao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhentao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieping","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jieping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jixing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinglin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qinglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengkui","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shengkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqiao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yanqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fun-ASR Technical Report"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:19:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:11:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T19:11:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:04:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-05T21:27:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-19T16:18:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:49:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, automatic speech recognition (ASR) has witnessed transformative advancements driven by three complementary paradigms: data scaling, model size scaling, and deep integration with large language models (LLMs). However, LLMs are prone to hallucination, which can significantly degrade user experience in real-world ASR applications. In this paper, we present Fun-ASR, a large-scale, LLM-based ASR system that synergistically combines massive data, large model capacity, LLM integration, and reinforcement learning to achieve state-of-the-art performance across diverse and complex speech recognition scenarios. Moreover, Fun-ASR is specifically optimized for practical deployment, with enhancements in streaming capability, noise robustness, code-switching, hotword customization, and satisfying other real-world application requirements. Experimental results show that while most LLM-based ASR systems achieve strong performance on open-source benchmarks, they often underperform on real industry evaluation sets. Thanks to production-oriented optimizations, Fun-ASR achieves state-of-the-art performance on real application datasets, demonstrating its effectiveness and robustness in practical settings. The code and models are accessible at https://github.com/FunAudioLLM/Fun-ASR ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Authors are listed in alphabetical order. Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12508","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:01Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-22T03:03:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12875","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12875","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12875","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binquan","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Binquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiting","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiyan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuantao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cangyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Cangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"jiaxu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, jiaxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiliang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Feiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LTA-thinker: Latent Thought-Augmented Training Framework for Large Language Models on Complex Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:27:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:35:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T13:58:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:55:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T12:50:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:42:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Complex Reasoning in Large Language Models can be dynamically optimized using Test-Time Scaling (TTS) to mitigate Overthinking. Methods such as Coconut, SoftCoT and its variant are effective in continuous latent space inference, the core bottleneck still lies in the efficient generation and utilization of high-quality Latent Thought. Drawing from the theory of SoftCoT++ that a larger variance in the generated Latent Thought distribution more closely approximates the golden truth distribution, we propose a Latent Thought-Augmented Training Framework--LTA-Thinker, which improves distributional variance and enhances reasoning performance from two perspectives. First, LTA-Thinker constructs a Latent Thought generation architecture based on a learnable prior. This architecture aims to increase the variance distribution of generated Latent Thought Vectors in order to simplify the overall structure and raise the performance ceiling. Second, LTA-Thinker introduces a distribution-based directional optimization paradigm that jointly constrains both distribution locality and distribution scale. This mechanism improves information efficiency and computational cost through a multi-objective co-training strategy, which combines standard Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) loss with two novel losses: Semantic Alignment Loss, which utilizes KL divergence to ensure that the Latent Thought is highly relevant to the semantics of the question; Reasoning Focus Loss, which utilizes a contrastive learning mechanism to guide the model to focus on the most critical reasoning steps. Experiments show that LTA-thinker achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance among various baselines and demonstrates a higher performance ceiling and better scaling effects."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12875","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:37Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-17T03:08:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12768","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12768","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12768","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Al-Habib","name":"Al-Habib, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zuping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdulrahman","familyName":"Noman","name":"Noman, Abdulrahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BATR-FST: Bi-Level Adaptive Token Refinement for Few-Shot Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:33:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:25:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ijcnn64981.2025.11227827","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) have shown significant promise in computer vision applications. However, their performance in few-shot learning is limited by challenges in refining token-level interactions, struggling with limited training data, and developing a strong inductive bias. Existing methods often depend on inflexible token matching or basic similarity measures, which limit the effective incorporation of global context and localized feature refinement. To address these challenges, we propose Bi-Level Adaptive Token Refinement for Few-Shot Transformers (BATR-FST), a two-stage approach that progressively improves token representations and maintains a robust inductive bias for few-shot classification. During the pre-training phase, Masked Image Modeling (MIM) provides Vision Transformers (ViTs) with transferable patch-level representations by recreating masked image regions, providing a robust basis for subsequent adaptation. In the meta-fine-tuning phase, BATR-FST incorporates a Bi-Level Adaptive Token Refinement module that utilizes Token Clustering to capture localized interactions, Uncertainty-Aware Token Weighting to prioritize dependable features, and a Bi-Level Attention mechanism to balance intra-cluster and inter-cluster relationships, thereby facilitating thorough token refinement. Furthermore, Graph Token Propagation ensures semantic consistency between support and query instances, while a Class Separation Penalty preserves different class borders, enhancing discriminative capability. Extensive experiments on three benchmark few-shot datasets demonstrate that BATR-FST achieves superior results in both 1-shot and 5-shot scenarios and improves the few-shot classification via transformers."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted for publication at the IEEE International Joint Conference on Neural Networks (IJCNN), Rome, Italy 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12768","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:48:03Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:48:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-17T03:08:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12748","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12748","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12748","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqi","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Tianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shufeng","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Shufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guangyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ruiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Leyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dezhi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Dezhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaocheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NEFT: A Unified Transformer Framework for Efficient Near-Field CSI Feedback in XL-MIMO Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:08:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:28:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T16:53:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T01:05:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T04:52:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:09:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extremely large-scale multiple-input multiple-output (XL-MIMO) systems, operating in the near-field region due to their massive antenna arrays, are key enablers of next-generation wireless communications but face significant challenges in channel state information (CSI) feedback. Deep learning has emerged as a powerful tool by learning compact channel features for feedback. However, existing methods struggle to capture the intricate structure of near-field CSI and incur prohibitive computational overhead on practical mobile devices. To overcome these limitations, we propose the near-field efficient feedback Transformer (NEFT) family for accurate near-field CSI feedback with reduced overhead under diverse hardware constraints. NEFT builds on a hierarchical vision Transformer backbone with progressive token reduction and multi-scale feature extraction, enabling compact and effective modeling of near-field channel characteristics. Furthermore, NEFT is extended with lightweight variants: NEFT-Compact applies multi-level knowledge distillation (KD) to reduce model complexity while preserving accuracy; NEFT-Hybrid adopts an attention-free CNN encoder to reduce encoder-side computation; and NEFT-Edge combines NEFT-Hybrid with KD to enable deployment on highly resource-constrained edge devices. Extensive simulations show that NEFT achieves a 15--21dB improvement in normalized mean-squared error over state-of-the-art methods, NEFT-Compact and NEFT-Edge reduce total FLOPs by 25-36% with negligible accuracy loss, while NEFT-Hybrid reduces encoder-side complexity by up to 64%, enabling deployment in highly asymmetric device scenarios. These results establish NEFT as a practical and scalable solution for near-field CSI feedback in XL-MIMO systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12748","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:46:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12718","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12718","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12718","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pukun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Pukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Longxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miaowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Miaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanqing","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Fanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haojian","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T06:21:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:26:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-26T04:04:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-27T01:22:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most existing spatial reasoning benchmarks focus on static or globally observable environments, failing to capture the challenges of long-horizon reasoning and memory utilization under partial observability and dynamic changes. We introduce two dynamic spatial benchmarks, locally observable maze navigation and match-2 elimination that systematically evaluate models' abilities in spatial understanding and adaptive planning when local perception, environment feedback, and global objectives are tightly coupled. Each action triggers structural changes in the environment, requiring continuous update of cognition and strategy. We further propose a subjective experience-based memory mechanism for cross-task experience transfer and validation. Experiments show that our benchmarks reveal key limitations of mainstream models in dynamic spatial reasoning and long-term memory, providing a comprehensive platform for future methodological advances. Our code and data are available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/EvoEmpirBench-143C/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by AAAI 2026, 29 pages, 3 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12718","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:53Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-27T03:16:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12247","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12247","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12247","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abigail R.","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Abigail R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuming","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh S.","familyName":"Muriki","name":"Muriki, Harsh S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeonju","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yeonju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Housley","name":"Housley, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew F.","familyName":"Sharkey","name":"Sharkey, Andrew F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rhuanito S.","familyName":"Ferrarezi","name":"Ferrarezi, Rhuanito S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongsheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modular, On-Site Solutions with Lightweight Anomaly Detection for Sustainable Nutrient Management in Agriculture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:14:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-26T15:30:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-27T01:55:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient nutrient management is critical for crop growth and sustainable resource consumption (e.g., nitrogen, energy). Current approaches require lengthy analyses, preventing real-time optimization; similarly, imaging facilitates rapid phenotyping but can be computationally intensive, preventing deployment under resource constraints. This study proposes a flexible, tiered pipeline for anomaly detection and status estimation (fresh weight, dry mass, and tissue nutrients), including a comprehensive energy analysis of approaches that span the efficiency-accuracy spectrum. Using a nutrient depletion experiment with three treatments (T1-100%, T2-50%, and T3-25% fertilizer strength) and multispectral imaging (MSI), we developed a hierarchical pipeline using an autoencoder (AE) for early warning. Further, we compared two status estimation modules of different complexity for more detailed analysis: vegetation index (VI) features with machine learning (Random Forest, RF) and raw whole-image deep learning (Vision Transformer, ViT). Results demonstrated high-efficiency anomaly detection (73% net detection of T3 samples 9 days after transplanting) at substantially lower energy than embodied energy in wasted nitrogen. The state estimation modules show trade-offs, with ViT outperforming RF on phosphorus and calcium estimation (R2 0.61 vs. 0.58, 0.48 vs. 0.35) at higher energy cost. With our modular pipeline, this work opens opportunities for edge diagnostics and practical opportunities for agricultural sustainability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12247","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:04Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-27T03:15:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12897","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12897","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12897","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jianfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixing","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Layer Vision Smoothing: Enhancing Visual Understanding via Sustained Focus on Key Objects in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:54:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:37:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-25T07:54:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:36:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) can accurately locate key objects in images, yet their attention to these objects tends to be very brief. Motivated by the hypothesis that sustained focus on key objects can improve LVLMs' visual capabilities, we propose Cross-Layer Vision Smoothing (CLVS). The core idea of CLVS is to incorporate a vision memory that smooths the attention distribution across layers. Specifically, we initialize this vision memory with position-unbiased visual attention in the first layer. In subsequent layers, the model's visual attention jointly considers the vision memory from previous layers, while the memory is updated iteratively, thereby maintaining smooth attention on key objects. Given that visual understanding primarily occurs in the early and middle layers of the model, we use uncertainty as an indicator of completed visual understanding and terminate the smoothing process accordingly. Experiments on four benchmarks across three LVLMs confirm the effectiveness and generalizability of our method. CLVS achieves state-of-the-art overall performance across a variety of visual understanding tasks and attains comparable results to the leading approaches on image captioning benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12897","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:51:08Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:51:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-26T03:24:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13161","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13161","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13161","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Tianyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tieyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tieyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huabin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huabin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaofan","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Chaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuxuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Weiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Video Large Language Models with Structured Multi-Video Collaborative Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:13:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:52:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T02:48:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-21T01:14:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the prosperity of the video language model, the current pursuit of comprehensive video reasoning is thwarted by the inherent spatio-temporal incompleteness within individual videos, resulting in hallucinations and inaccuracies. A promising solution is to augment the reasoning performance with multiple related videos. However, video tokens are numerous and contain redundant information, so directly feeding the relevant video data into a large language model to enhance responses could be counterproductive. To address this challenge, we propose a multi-video collaborative framework for video language models. For efficient and flexible video representation, we establish a Video Structuring Module to represent the video's knowledge as a spatio-temporal graph. Based on the structured video representation, we design the Graph Fusion Module to fuse the structured knowledge and valuable information from related videos into the augmented graph node tokens. Finally, we construct an elaborate multi-video structured prompt to integrate the graph, visual, and textual tokens as the input to the large language model. Extensive experiments substantiate the effectiveness of our framework, showcasing its potential as a promising avenue for advancing video language models. Code will be open-sourced at https://github.com/ziHoHe/SMV-CR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13161","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:57:19Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:57:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-21T03:02:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12527","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12527","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12527","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjeda","familyName":"Akter","name":"Akter, Sanjeda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibne Farabi","familyName":"Shihab","name":"Shihab, Ibne Farabi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anuj","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Anuj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Selective Risk Certification for LLM Outputs via Information-Lift Statistics: PAC-Bayes, Robustness, and Skeleton Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T00:05:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:12:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T01:55:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:15:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T05:02:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-20T01:25:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models often produce confident but incorrect outputs, creating a critical need for reliable uncertainty quantification with formal abstention guarantees. We introduce information-lift certificates that compare model probabilities to a skeleton baseline, accumulating evidence through sub-gamma PAC-Bayes bounds that remain valid under heavy-tailed distributions where standard concentration inequalities fail. On eight diverse datasets, our method achieves 77.0\\% coverage at 2\\% risk, outperforming recent baselines by 10.0 percentage points on average. In high-stakes scenarios, we block 96\\% of critical errors compared to 18-31\\% for entropy-based methods. While our frequency-based certification does not guarantee severity-weighted safety and depends on skeleton quality, performance degrades gracefully under distributional shifts, making the approach practical for real-world deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12527","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:42:28Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:42:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-20T03:13:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12443","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12443","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12443","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sparsh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Sparsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamalavasan","familyName":"Kamalakkannan","name":"Kamalakkannan, Kamalavasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxim","familyName":"Moraru","name":"Moraru, Maxim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Galen","familyName":"Shipman","name":"Shipman, Galen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Diehl","name":"Diehl, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Legacy Fortran to Portable Kokkos: An Autonomous Agentic AI Workflow"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:50:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:08:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T15:29:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:49:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-17T03:45:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:26:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scientific applications continue to rely on legacy Fortran codebases originally developed for homogeneous, CPU-based systems. As High-Performance Computing (HPC) shifts toward heterogeneous GPU-accelerated architectures, many accelerators lack native Fortran bindings, creating an urgent need to modernize legacy codes for portability. Frameworks like Kokkos provide performance portability and a single-source C++ abstraction, but manual Fortran-to-Kokkos porting demands significant expertise and time. Large language models (LLMs) have shown promise in source-to-source code generation, yet their use in fully autonomous workflows for translating and optimizing parallel code remains largely unexplored, especially for performance portability across diverse hardware. This paper presents an agentic AI workflow where specialized LLM \"agents\" collaborate to translate, validate, compile, run, test, debug, and optimize Fortran kernels into portable Kokkos C++ programs. Results show the pipeline modernizes a range of benchmark kernels, producing performance-portable Kokkos codes across hardware partitions. Paid OpenAI models such as GPT-5 and o4-mini-high executed the workflow for only a few U.S. dollars, generating optimized codes that surpassed Fortran baselines, whereas open-source models like Llama4-Maverick often failed to yield functional codes. This work demonstrates the feasibility of agentic AI for Fortran-to-Kokkos transformation and offers a pathway for autonomously modernizing legacy scientific applications to run portably and efficiently on diverse supercomputers. It further highlights the potential of LLM-driven agentic systems to perform structured, domain-specific reasoning tasks in scientific and systems-oriented applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 6 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12443","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:33Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:17:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12503","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12503","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12503","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Corey M.","familyName":"Abramson","name":"Abramson, Corey M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tara","familyName":"Prendergast","name":"Prendergast, Tara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuofan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhuofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Dohan","name":"Dohan, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Qualitative Research in an Era of AI: A Pragmatic Approach to Data Analysis, Workflow, and Computation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T23:12:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:11:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T18:59:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T17:57:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:51:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-11T18:36:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-12T02:04:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-12T14:08:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:48:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computational developments--particularly artificial intelligence--are reshaping social scientific research and raise new questions for in-depth methods such as ethnography and qualitative interviewing. Building on classic debates about computers in qualitative data analysis (QDA), we revisit possibilities and dangers in an era of automation, Large Language Model (LLM) chatbots, and 'big data.' We introduce a typology of contemporary approaches to using computers in qualitative research: streamlining workflows, scaling up projects, hybrid analytical methods, the sociology of computation, and technological rejection. Drawing from scaled team ethnographies and solo research integrating computational social science (CSS), we describe methodological choices across study lifecycles, from literature reviews through data collection, coding, text retrieval, and representation. We argue that new technologies hold potential to address longstanding methodological challenges when deployed with knowledge, purpose, and ethical commitment. Yet a pragmatic approach--moving beyond technological optimism and dismissal--is essential given rapidly changing tools that are both generative and dangerous. Computation now saturates research infrastructure, from algorithmic literature searches to scholarly metrics, making computational literacy a core methodological competence in and beyond sociology. We conclude that when used carefully and transparently, contemporary computational tools can meaningfully expand--rather than displace--the irreducible insights of qualitative research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"FORTHCOMING: Abramson, Corey M., Tara Prendergast, Zhuofan Li, Daniel Dohan. 2026 (forthcoming). \"Qualitative Research in an Era of AI: A Pragmatic Approach to Data Analysis, Workflow, and Computation\". Annual Review of Sociology. pre-print, methodology, workflow article"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12503","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:54Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-13T03:08:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12728","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12728","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12728","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeongsol","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jeongsol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanseok","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Chanseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongin","familyName":"You","name":"You, Jongin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Chul","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jong Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mooseok","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Mooseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalizable Holographic Reconstruction via Amplitude-Only Diffusion Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T06:36:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:27:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T04:28:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:22:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T08:07:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-12T01:30:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Phase retrieval in inline holography is a fundamental yet ill-posed inverse problem due to the nonlinear coupling between amplitude and phase in coherent imaging. We present a novel off-the-shelf solution that leverages a diffusion model trained solely on object amplitude to recover both amplitude and phase from diffraction intensities. Using a predictor-corrector sampling framework with separate likelihood gradients for amplitude and phase, our method enables complex field reconstruction without requiring ground-truth phase data for training. We validate the proposed approach through extensive simulations and experiments, demonstrating robust generalization across diverse object shapes, imaging system configurations, and modalities, including lensless setups. Notably, a diffusion prior trained on simple amplitude data (e.g., polystyrene beads) successfully reconstructs complex biological tissue structures, highlighting the method's adaptability. This framework provides a cost-effective, generalizable solution for nonlinear inverse problems in computational imaging, and establishes a foundation for broader coherent imaging applications beyond holography."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Keywords: Diffusion model, phase retrieval, inline-holography, inverse problem"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12728","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:07Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-12T03:27:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":281,"totalPages":12,"page":3},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=3\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=4\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29"}}